Perhaps this could be something like a branch; imagine it like this:

Level 0: Chasm top, dungeon level 5 for example (or perhaps
  at minend-1). Usually a bridge across it (same level of peril as
  a long drawbridge), or it doesn't fully block progress across
  the level.
Level 1: Middle of the chasm; there are ledges with caves/rooms off
  of them, flying enemies (lots of gargoyles perhaps).
Level 2: Intersection with DOD, around level 18
Level 3: Ledges and rooms, presumably some unique challenge and reward.
Level X: Falling from level 3 lands you in the VotD, or some random
  genehennom level, fatally...

The point of this is, if you fall in the chasm you drop to the
next level down -- but you start over a chasm square, so you get
one turn to do something before you drop down again.

In that one turn you could put on a ring of levitation, cast the
spell, polymorph yourself into something that flies.. but you can
also move to a ledge space:

  .^.
 .^^^
 ^^^@.
 ^^^^
 ^

Here the hero is over a chasm, but can move east and smack down
on the ledge there to avoid falling another level.  Of course
there might not be anything accessible from that ledge, in which
case the hero is in a quandry.

There could be more levels to the chasm with stairways between
them; perhaps when you fall you go down 1-3 levels at random
making it difficult to stop where you like.

A flying creature or hero can use "<" and ">" in chasm squares
to go up and down levels.

With a bullwhip (and much skill) or a grappling hook the hero
could perhaps hook on to something 2 squares away, increasing
survivability when plunging down the chasm.. depending on the
design of the levels.

Burdened alone is not a good measure of the total weight of
the hero since it depends on strength. You'd probably have to
calculate some total weight and then communicate danger with
messages that the bridge is creaking dangerously.

Also if it's a rope bridge we're talking about, that's
another terrain type, really...

Possibly it's better to abstract this out; a floor square next
to a chasm square is "unstable" depending on how many chasm
squares are next to it. Other actions besides walking can make
it collapse (digging down for example: 100% chance). Then
a natural bridge can be just a line of normal floor squares.

